 "notes": "**How the matching rule was iterated.** The first hand-read samples found artefacts well above 10%, so the oracle and matcher were changed and the samples redrawn:\n\n- Go FN, first sample: 9 of 20 (45%) were dynamic-dispatch edges. RTA lists every possible target of an interface call or function-value call, so lidx missing one of them is not evidence of an error. Dispatch edges now leave the headline recall and get their own column.\n- TypeScript FN, first sample: 5 of 20 (25%) had a callee that is a function nested inside another function. lidx indexes no such symbol, so the edge cannot exist in its graph. Callees lidx does not index now join the caller capture gap.\n- Oracle bugs found while reading failures (not sampled): Go range-over-func loop bodies and generic instances were missing from the graph, and RTA reported unreachable functions as judgeable callers; TypeScript callees typed through an interface or function type, JSX components, tagged templates and parameters; Rust tuple-struct and enum-variant constructors, trait-method dispatch; interface and const of the same name treated as two declarations. All are fixed in the oracles or the matcher.\n\nThe final samples (this section) were drawn after those changes: 120 items, none an artefact. For 40 items per language a rate of 0 has a 95% Wilson upper bound of 8.8%, under the 10% bar. Two caveats on reading the table: (1) the sample is stratified only by random draw, so rare categories (e.g. the handful of Go closure-callee FPs) are unlikely to appear; (2) verdicts are one reader's judgement from the source at the call site, recorded with a note per item.\n\nCategories that dominate each language drive the next fixes: see the per-cell FN/FP category lines above and `results/lists/`.",
